"Agent swarm" now names three different designs: a model that spawns and manages its own army of sub-agents, a team of agents that share a task list and message each other, and a router that hands a conversation from one specialist to the next. Each has a different cost, a different failure mode and a different best use. Most pages on the subject pick one meaning and treat the others as noise, which is how people end up paying for a swarm when a single subagent would have done the job.

This guide defines the term, separates Claude Code subagents from Claude Code agent teams using Anthropic's own docs, and compares ten tools on prices and limits checked on November 26, 2026. The short version: Claude Code is the practical default for developers, Kimi Agent Swarm is the hosted swarm with the largest published ceiling, Hermes Kanban is the open source option built around an auditable board, and the SDKs (OpenAI Agents SDK, Google ADK, CrewAI, Agency Swarm, Swarms) are where you go when you are building your own product.

{% youtube id="v7IJ8JNUNQ8" title="Meet Kimi K2.6 Agent Swarm" /%}

*Kimi's own launch video for the K2.6 Agent Swarm (title and channel "Kimi AI" confirmed through YouTube oEmbed on November 26, 2026). Numbers in it are vendor-reported.*

## Short answer: quick picks

- **Best overall for developers in a terminal:** [Claude Code](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents). Subagents are a normal feature. Agent teams are experimental and off by default.
- **Best hosted swarm with no setup:** [Kimi Agent Swarm](https://www.kimi.com/agent-swarm). Vendor-reported ceiling of 300 sub-agents and more than 4,000 tool calls in one task. Needs a paid Kimi plan.
- **Best open source for durable, auditable multi-agent work:** [Hermes Kanban](https://hermes-agent.nousresearch.com/docs/user-guide/features/kanban). A SQLite task board where each task runs in its own managed worker (MIT, 266,338 GitHub stars on the Hermes Agent repo as of November 26, 2026).
- **Best free SDK for handoff routing:** [OpenAI Agents SDK](https://openai.github.io/openai-agents-python/handoffs/) (MIT, 30,890 stars).
- **Best known open source role-based framework:** [CrewAI](https://docs.crewai.com/en/introduction) (MIT, 61,301 stars, free tier on its hosted platform).
- **Best if you already live on Google Cloud tooling:** [Google ADK](https://github.com/google/adk-python) (Apache-2.0, 22,364 stars).
- **Best for non-developers who want a swarm inside a desktop app:** [Kimi Work](https://www.kimi.com/en/products/kimi-work), a beta desktop agent for Mac and Windows that can switch into Agent Swarm mode.
- **Best to read, not to depend on:** [swarm-ide](https://github.com/chmod777john/swarm-ide). A neat community visualiser with no declared license.

{% callout type="info" %}
**Honest caveat.** There is no neutral benchmark that compares these tools against each other, and we did not benchmark any of them. Every performance number below is labelled vendor-reported. Prices, limits and star counts are dated November 26, 2026 and will move.
{% /callout %}

## What an agent swarm means

Start with what everyone agrees on. A single agent works through a task one step at a time inside one context window. A swarm splits the work across several agents, each with its own context, and some mechanism combines the results. The word "swarm" is marketing more than engineering, and the vendors use it loosely:

- **Kimi** calls its feature Agent Swarm. Its help center describes a "scale-out" architecture that coordinates large numbers of sub-agents without predefined roles or manually designed workflows, and says Kimi K2.5 introduced it on January 27, 2026. The behavior is trained with a method Kimi calls PARL (Parallel-Agent Reinforcement Learning).
- **Claude Code** got a post from @bcherny on February 5, 2026 reading "Out now: Teams, aka. Agent Swarms in Claude Code" (embedded below). Anthropic's own docs use the plainer word "agent teams".
- **OpenAI Swarm** was a small educational library built around handoffs between agents. Its README now says it has been replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK and recommends migrating for production use.
- **Swarms** (kyegomez) and **Agency Swarm** use the word in their names and ship their own orchestration patterns.

So in practice you will meet three patterns:

1. **Fan-out and gather (orchestrator plus workers).** A lead agent splits a job, workers run in parallel, the lead merges. Workers usually do not talk to each other. Kimi Agent Swarm, Claude Code subagents, Hermes `delegate_task` and the orchestrator-worker flow in Agency Swarm all fit here.
2. **Peer team with shared state.** Workers claim tasks from a shared list or board and can message each other. Claude Code agent teams and Hermes Kanban fit here.
3. **Handoff chain.** One agent is active at a time and transfers control to a specialist who takes over the conversation. The OpenAI Agents SDK and the handoff flow in Agency Swarm fit here.

Sequential pipelines (CrewAI's default process, ADK's sequential template workflows) are a fourth, quieter pattern: no parallelism, just specialised steps in order. They are often what people actually need.

### The cost nobody puts in the headline

Anthropic's agent teams page says teams "add coordination overhead and use significantly more tokens than a single session", and that for sequential tasks, same-file edits or work with many dependencies, a single session or subagents are more effective. The @bcherny launch post says the same thing in fewer words: experimental, and a lot of tokens. Hermes documents the same shape for its own delegation: a nested tree with `max_spawn_depth` 3 and `max_concurrent_children` 3 can reach 27 concurrent leaf agents, and "each extra level multiplies spend".

A swarm is a way to buy speed and breadth with tokens. If the task is sequential, touches one file or has many dependencies, a single agent is usually the better buy.

## Claude Code subagents vs agent teams

This is the comparison people search for, and it is easy to get wrong because the two features share vocabulary. Everything in this section comes from Anthropic's docs pages for [subagents](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/sub-agents) and [agent teams](https://code.claude.com/docs/en/agent-teams), fetched on November 26, 2026.

{% figure src="https://mintcdn.com/claude-code/nsvRFSDNfpSU5nT7/images/subagents-vs-agent-teams-light.png?fit=max&auto=format&n=nsvRFSDNfpSU5nT7&q=85&s=2f8db9b4f3705dd3ab931fbe2d96e42a" alt="Diagram comparing subagent and agent team architectures. Subagents are spawned by the main agent, do work, and report results back. Agent teams coordinate through a shared task list, with teammates communicating directly with each other." caption="Anthropic's own diagram of the difference: subagents report back to the main agent, teammates share a task list and talk to each other. Source: Anthropic Claude Code docs" /%}

| | Subagents | Agent teams |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Status | Regular feature, built-in types plus your own | Experimental, disabled by default |
| Where they run | Inside one session, each with its own context window | Separate Claude Code instances, each with its own context window |
| Communication | Report a result back to the caller; named agents can also message each other through `SendMessage` | Teammates message each other directly, and you can talk to any teammate |
| Coordination | The main agent manages everything | A shared task list with automatic dependency unblocking |
| Token cost | Lower: only a summary returns to the main context | Higher: every teammate has its own context window and usage scales with active teammates |
| Best for | Focused jobs where only the result matters | Work that needs discussion, such as competing debugging hypotheses or parallel review |
| How to start | Ask Claude, or add a markdown file under `.claude/agents/` | Set `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1`, then ask for a team |

A few details from the docs that change the decision:

- **Subagent definitions are plain markdown with frontmatter.** The body is the system prompt. Claude Code discovers project subagents by walking up from the working directory through every `.claude/agents/` folder, and the definition closest to your working directory wins when names collide.
- **Built-in subagents exist.** Explore (read-only codebase search) and Plan (research during plan mode) start without your CLAUDE.md files and the git status snapshot, to keep research fast and cheap.
- **Subagents can nest, teams cannot.** By default a subagent can spawn subagents of its own, up to three layers below the main conversation. The docs give a reviewer that dispatches a verifier per finding as the example, and `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_SUBAGENT_SPAWN_DEPTH` changes the limit (set it to 1 to turn nesting off). Agent teams have no nested teams: teammates cannot spawn their own teammates.
- **Turning teams on changes ordinary delegation.** While agent teams are enabled, a subagent that Claude names launches as a teammate, so a team can form even if you did not ask for one. Spawning teammates also needs an interactive session, so `claude -p` does not spawn them.
- **Teams have real limits today.** The docs list no session resumption for in-process teammates, task status that can lag, slow shutdown, one team per session, no nested teams, a fixed lead, and split panes that need tmux or iTerm2.

Rule of thumb from those docs: use subagents when you want quick workers that report back, and teams when the workers need to challenge each other. Start with subagents.

