## The ten tools

Each section below follows the same order: what it is, who it is for, key features, pros, cons, benefits, pricing, free tier, limits, a standout use case, who should skip it, and a setup snippet where one is useful. Star counts come from the GitHub API on November 26, 2026. "Vendor-reported" means the number comes from the vendor and we did not reproduce it.

### 1. Claude Code subagents and agent teams

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/claude-code.png" name="Claude Code" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Anthropic's terminal coding agent, with two multi-agent features. Subagents are specialised workers that run inside your session and hand back a summary. Agent teams are several full Claude Code instances that share a task list and message each other. Both are documented on code.claude.com.

**Who it is for.** Developers who already use Claude Code and want to split research, review or implementation work without leaving the terminal.

**Key features (from Anthropic's docs).**

- Custom subagents are markdown files with frontmatter, kept in `.claude/agents/`, and Claude uses each subagent's description to decide when to delegate.
- Built-in Explore and Plan subagents run read-only research without polluting your main context.
- Nested subagents: up to three layers by default, adjustable with `CLAUDE_CODE_MAX_SUBAGENT_SPAWN_DEPTH`.
- Agent teams: a shared task list, automatic unblocking of dependent tasks, direct messages between teammates, and an optional plan approval step before a teammate starts implementing.
- Two display modes for teams: in-process (any terminal) or split panes (tmux or iTerm2), controlled by the `teammateMode` setting.

**Pros**

- Subagents are a regular feature with the most detailed first-party documentation of any tool here.
- You can message any teammate directly and watch their transcripts from the agent panel.
- Subagents keep the main context clean, because only a summary returns.

**Cons**

- Agent teams are experimental, disabled by default, and Anthropic lists known limitations: no resumption of in-process teammates, task status that can lag, one team per session, no nested teams and a fixed lead.
- Teams cost significantly more tokens than a single session, by Anthropic's own statement.
- The public anthropics/claude-code GitHub repo (157,195 stars as of November 26, 2026) lists no license, so do not assume you can fork or reuse the code.

**Benefits for you.** If your work is code, this is the lowest-friction way to try parallel agents. Start with subagents, and only turn on teams for work where teammates should challenge each other.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, claude.com/pricing).** Free $0 (Claude Code: No), Pro $17 per month with the annual discount ($200 billed up front) or $20 billed monthly, Max $100 (5x Pro usage), Ultra $200 (10x Pro weekly usage). Max and Ultra also list $100 and $200 of monthly API credits.

**Free tier.** None for Claude Code: the pricing page lists it as No on the Free plan.

**Limits.** Subagent descriptions that together exceed 15,000 tokens (built-ins excluded) trigger a startup warning. Teams: one per session, no nesting.

**Standout use case.** Anthropic's own example for teams is a design review: three teammates take UX, technical architecture and devil's advocate roles at the same time.

**Skip it if** you need a headless swarm inside a product you ship (use an SDK instead), or you cannot spend extra tokens on a feature that its own docs call experimental.

**Setup.** A subagent is a file. A team needs one environment variable:

```json
{
  "env": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS": "1"
  },
  "teammateMode": "auto"
}
```

Save that in `settings.json`, then describe the team you want in plain language, for example "spawn three teammates to review this PR, one on security, one on performance, one on test coverage".

### 2. Kimi Agent Swarm

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/kimi-agent-swarm.png" name="Kimi Agent Swarm" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A hosted multi-agent mode inside Kimi (Moonshot AI). You describe a large task, an orchestrator decides how many sub-agents to create, and the results come back as one deliverable. Kimi's help center says it launched with K2.5 on January 27, 2026, was upgraded with K2.6 on April 20, 2026, and is now powered by Kimi K3.

**Who it is for.** Researchers and analysts with wide, parallel jobs: scan 100 niches, read 100 documents, collect 200 essays.

**Key features (vendor-reported by Kimi).**

- Up to 300 sub-agents at once and more than 4,000 tool calls in a single task (K2.6 figures from the help center).
- The orchestrator creates sub-agents on the fly, without predefined roles or a hand-built workflow.
- Progress of each agent is visible while it runs, and outputs include code projects, charts and tables, and Word, PDF, Markdown or PPT documents.
- Kimi says the architecture cuts the critical steps needed in large-scale search by 3x to 4.5x versus a single agent. We could not reproduce that.

**Pros**

- No setup, no framework and no API key to manage.
- The largest published ceiling in this comparison.
- Kimi's help center gives concrete use cases, such as 100-niche research and multi-role expert reviews.

**Cons**

- Closed product (though Kimi K2.6 is described as open-sourced).
- Concurrency is capped by plan: 2, 4, 8 and 8 concurrent subtasks on Plus, Pro, Max and Ultra.
- All membership features share one monthly credit pool with no rollover, so a big swarm run can use up credits that other Kimi features also need.
- The help center carries a Beta marker for the product entry point.

**Benefits for you.** If your task is "do the same research 100 times, then merge", you buy parallelism with credits and write no code.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, kimi.ai pricing pages).** Billed annually, the monthly equivalents are Plus $15, Pro $31, Max $79 and Ultra $159. Billed monthly they are $19, $39, $99 and $199. Annual totals are $180, $372, $948 and $1,908. Agent credits scale from Basic on Free to More, 2x, 5x and 10x.

**Free tier.** The Free plan is $0, but its swarm concurrency cell is blank in the plan table, so treat Agent Swarm as a paid feature. Plus is described as covering "Coding with Kimi Code, research, office tasks, and Agent Swarm".

**Limits.** Swarm concurrent subtasks: 2 (Plus), 4 (Pro), 8 (Max), 8 (Ultra). Regular agent concurrent tasks are 1, 2, 2, 4 and 4 across Free to Ultra.

**Standout use case.** Kimi's own example: find the top three creators in each of 100 YouTube niches, with one search specialist per niche, ending in a structured table and charts.

**Skip it if** your data cannot leave your machine, you need a fixed audit trail of who did what, or the task is sequential.

