### 3. Hermes Kanban multi-agent board

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/hermes-kanban.png" name="Hermes Kanban" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A durable task board in Hermes Agent (Nous Research) shared across all your Hermes profiles. Every task is a row in a local SQLite file at `~/.hermes/kanban.db`, and every handoff between agents is a row anyone can read. Its docs frame it as the alternative to "fragile in-process subagent swarms".

**Who it is for.** People who run several named Hermes profiles and want work to survive restarts, pause for human input and be picked up later by a different role.

**Key features (Hermes docs).**

- Task statuses: triage, todo, ready, running, blocked, review, done, archived.
- Parent to child links: the dispatcher promotes a task from todo to ready when all parents are done.
- Comments are the inter-agent protocol, and a re-spawned worker reads the full thread.
- Three workspace kinds: scratch (deleted on completion), a shared directory, or a git worktree for coding tasks.
- A dispatcher loop (every 60 seconds by default) reclaims stale claims and crashed workers.
- Multiple boards, each with its own SQLite file, plus a dashboard Kanban tab and a `kanban_*` toolset the agents use.

**Pros**

- Auditable: the docs say every handoff is a row, and `hermes kanban gc` prunes old events once tasks are done or archived.
- Human in the loop at any point through comments and unblocking.
- MIT licensed, and the Hermes Agent repo had 266,338 GitHub stars as of November 26, 2026.

**Cons**

- It is a coordination layer for Hermes profiles, not a general SDK for your own product.
- It is single-machine by design (a local SQLite board).
- More moving parts than a one-shot subagent call: profiles, a gateway-embedded dispatcher, workspaces.

**Benefits for you.** You get a visible, restartable pipeline (research, write, review, retry) instead of an opaque tree of children that disappears when the session ends.

**Pricing.** Free software. You pay your own model provider. (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Fully free to run.

**Limits.** Hermes documents a separate in-session tool, `delegate_task`, that can fan out child agents. Its depth is flat by default (`max_spawn_depth: 1`), and the docs warn that depth 3 with 3 children each can reach 27 concurrent leaf agents. Two Hermes docs pages disagree about the default child concurrency: the configuration reference says 3 per batch, while the delegation page says 10 tasks by default. Check your own config before relying on either number.

**Standout use case.** Nous Research's launch thread describes agents that claim tasks from a board, work in parallel, and hand off when blocked, with you watching and unblocking from one view instead of juggling terminals.

**Skip it if** you want a hosted swarm with no local setup, or you are building a product for end users.

**Setup.** Raise nesting only on purpose:

```yaml
delegation:
  max_spawn_depth: 2   # children may spawn leaf grandchildren
  max_concurrent_children: 3
```

