### 4. OpenAI Agents SDK handoffs

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/openai-agents-sdk.png" name="OpenAI Agents SDK" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** OpenAI's open source Python SDK for building agents. Its multi-agent story has two primitives: handoffs, where a triage agent passes the conversation to a specialist, and agents as tools, where a manager agent calls specialists but keeps control. It is also the production successor to OpenAI's experimental Swarm library, whose README tells you to migrate.

**Who it is for.** Developers building their own product who want a small, explicit routing primitive rather than a large framework.

**Key features (docs).**

- A handoff is shown to the model as a tool, so a handoff to `Refund Agent` becomes `transfer_to_refund_agent`.
- Agents take a `handoffs` list, and an optional `handoff_description` hints when the model should pick that route. `handoff()` adds overrides and input filters.
- `Agent.as_tool()` for the manager pattern, where one agent owns the final answer.
- Human-in-the-loop approvals through `needs_approval`, covering handoffs and nested agent tools.
- Sessions, tracing and sandbox agents are documented alongside.

**Pros**

- Clear guidance on when to use which pattern: the orchestration page says handoffs fit when the specialist should answer directly, and agents as tools fit when one agent should own the final answer.
- MIT licensed, 30,890 stars as of November 26, 2026, version 0.26.0 on PyPI.
- Handoffs and agent-tools can be combined.

**Cons**

- You write the orchestration. There is no board, no shared task list and no built-in swarm of parallel workers.
- Pre-1.0 version number, so expect API movement.
- The SDK is free, but model usage is billed by your provider.

**Benefits for you.** The simplest mental model here: one active agent at a time, explicit routes, and approvals you can audit.

**Pricing.** The SDK is free (MIT). You pay the model API you point it at. (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Yes for the library. There is no pricing page for the SDK itself.

**Limits.** Requires Python 3.10 or newer.

**Standout use case.** OpenAI's own example: a customer support app where a triage agent routes to order status, refunds or FAQ specialists.

**Skip it if** you need parallel fan-out with minimal code (Kimi, Claude Code or a framework with workflow types will get you there faster) or non-Python runtimes.

**Setup.**

```python
from agents import Agent, Runner

billing = Agent(
    name="Billing agent",
    instructions="Answer billing questions.",
    handoff_description="Billing and refund questions",
)
triage = Agent(
    name="Triage agent",
    instructions="Route the user to the right specialist.",
    handoffs=[billing],
)
result = Runner.run_sync(triage, "I was charged twice.")
print(result.final_output)
```

### 5. CrewAI

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/crewai.png" name="CrewAI" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** An open source Python framework where you define agents with roles and tasks, then assemble them into a crew. It also sells a hosted platform for running and governing agents.

**Who it is for.** Teams who think in roles ("researcher", "writer", "reviewer") and want sequential or manager-led pipelines with a path to a hosted platform.

**Key features (docs).**

- A crew's `process` is sequential by default, or hierarchical, which requires either `manager_llm` or `manager_agent`.
- Optional planning, memory and caching settings on the crew.
- Crews can be defined in code or as JSON definitions that name a manager agent.
- A hosted platform with a Basic tier and an Enterprise tier.

**Pros**

- Role-based vocabulary that non-engineers can read.
- MIT licensed, the most starred of the SDKs and frameworks here at 61,301 stars (November 26, 2026), version 1.15.45 on PyPI.
- A free hosted tier to try.

**Cons**

- The default process is sequential, which is a pipeline rather than a swarm. Parallel behavior takes deliberate design.
- Hierarchical mode needs you to supply a manager model.
- Platform execution caps apply on the free tier.

**Benefits for you.** If you want to hand a business process to a small team of role-playing agents and can accept a framework's opinions, CrewAI is the easiest on-ramp.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, crewai.com/pricing).** Basic: Free, with 50 workflow executions per month. Enterprise: custom, described as adding governance, connectors and a 45-day onboarding. The open source library is free.

**Free tier.** Yes: the library, plus Basic on the platform.

**Limits.** Python at least 3.10 and below 3.14 per PyPI. 50 executions a month on Basic.

**Standout use case.** CrewAI's quickstart builds a Flow that sets a topic, runs a crew with one researcher agent that uses web search, and ends with a markdown report on disk. It is a small example, but it shows the framework's shape: Flows for control, crews for agent work.

**Skip it if** you need low-level control over every handoff, or a visual, restartable task board.

**Setup.**

```python
from crewai import Crew, Process

crew = Crew(
    agents=[researcher, writer, reviewer],
    tasks=[research_task, write_task, review_task],
    process=Process.hierarchical,
    manager_llm="<your-model-id>",   # required for hierarchical
)
result = crew.kickoff()
```

{% figure src="https://images.ctfassets.net/rmv9a2eqdsyw/26ld4zbjbNkeg6KGxkyLXE/7d686ce386ed7eb61f3d0f1d3709949c/og-image__2_.png" alt="CrewAI social preview image from crewai.com." caption="CrewAI's own social preview image, served from the crewai.com site. Source: CrewAI" /%}

### 6. Google ADK multi-agent workflows

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/google-adk.png" name="Google ADK" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** Google's open source Agent Development Kit. Its docs now organise multi-agent work under "Workflows: multi-agent, multi-node applications", and offer four ways to build them. The Python package is `google-adk` (version 2.15.1 on PyPI as of November 26, 2026).

**Who it is for.** Teams that want a choice between fixed, predictable control flow and a model that decides who to call, and who are comfortable in Python.

**Key features (ADK docs).**

- Graph-based workflows (ADK 2.0 and higher): agents and deterministic nodes composed into an execution graph with decision branching.
- Dynamic workflows (ADK 2.0 and higher): the same mix, driven by ordinary program logic.
- Collaborative workflows (ADK 2.0 and higher): a single agent acts as a dynamic coordinator over a set of specified sub-agents.
- Template workflows built from `BaseAgent`: fixed sequences, loops and parallel execution.
- An experimental Agent Routing feature that picks between agents at runtime for fallback, A/B testing and auto-routing.

**Pros**

- The docs state the trade-off in plain terms: workflows buy predictability, reliability and structure, which is the opposite pitch from an autonomous swarm.
- Apache-2.0, 22,364 stars (November 26, 2026), active push the same day.
- Template workflows give you real parallel execution without a model deciding it.

**Cons**

- Several of the most relevant workflow types need ADK 2.0 or higher, so read the version note before copying older tutorials.
- Python 3.11 or newer for the Python package.
- The docs site moved paths during our research: the older `agents/multi-agents` URL on adk.dev now redirects to the Workflows page.

**Benefits for you.** If you want the "swarm" effect but need to explain to a reviewer exactly what runs in what order, template and graph workflows give you that.

**Pricing.** The kit is free. You pay for the models and any Google Cloud services you deploy on. (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Yes for the library. Model usage depends on your provider.

**Limits.** Agent Routing is marked experimental. Collaborative, graph and dynamic workflows are 2.0 and higher.

**Standout use case.** A coordinator agent that delegates to named sub-agents, which is the closest ADK gets to a fan-out swarm, while sequential and parallel templates cover the fixed pipelines.

**Skip it if** you want a no-code product, or you need a swarm that designs its own topology at run time.

**Setup.**

```bash
pip install google-adk
```

{% figure src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/1/google/adk-python" alt="GitHub social card for the google/adk-python repository." caption="GitHub's generated social card for the google/adk-python repository. Source: GitHub, google/adk-python" /%}

### 7. Agency Swarm

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/agency-swarm.png" name="Agency Swarm" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** An open source agent orchestration framework by VRSEN (Arsenii Shatokhin), built on the OpenAI Agents SDK and Responses API. You define agents with roles, tools and explicit communication flows, then group them as an "agency".

**Who it is for.** Developers who want swarms with hand-drawn topology: who may talk to whom, and in what style.

**Key features (docs).**

- Communication flows are declared as tuples in `communication_flows`, so the topology is explicit.
- Two documented flow types. Handoff transfers control completely to another agent. Orchestrator-worker has one agent assign tasks to others through the `SendMessage` mechanism and compile the answers, with control returning to the orchestrator each time.
- Pydantic-based type validation and error correction for tools.
- Agents communicate "based on their own descriptions and nothing else", per the overview page.
- Custom Python tools for external APIs.

**Pros**

- The communication-flows page is unusually honest about trade-offs: it recommends handoffs for sequential work with tight user feedback, and orchestrator-worker for independent, not-very-high-stakes steps.
- MIT licensed, 4,643 stars (November 26, 2026), version 1.11.0 on PyPI.
- Sits on a mainstream SDK rather than its own runtime.

**Cons**

- Python 3.12 or newer.
- Fewer GitHub stars than CrewAI or the OpenAI SDK it builds on.
- You decide the topology up front: the docs say communication flows are "uniform" and can be defined in any way you want, which is control, but also a design job.

**Benefits for you.** You get both the handoff chain and the fan-out pattern from one framework, and the topology is written down in code where a teammate can review it.

**Pricing.** Free framework (MIT). Model costs go to your provider. (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Yes.

**Limits.** Python 3.12 or newer.

**Standout use case.** The docs pair each flow with a use case: a planner and script writer working with tight feedback (handoff), versus news aggregation across many sources (orchestrator-worker).

**Skip it if** you want a hosted product, a visual board or a fully autonomous swarm.

**Setup.**

```python
from agency_swarm import Agency, Handoff

agency = Agency(
    triage_agent,  # entry point
    communication_flows=[
        (triage_agent, billing_specialist, Handoff),
        (triage_agent, technical_specialist, Handoff),
    ],
)
```

### 8. Swarms (kyegomez)

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/swarms.png" name="Swarms" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** An open source Python framework (kyegomez/swarms) plus a hosted API at swarms.ai. It ships a catalogue of prebuilt multi-agent structures. This is a different project from OpenAI's old Swarm library and from Agency Swarm.

**Who it is for.** Developers who want to try many orchestration shapes quickly and swap between them.

**Key features (README).** Its architecture table lists ten structures: SequentialWorkflow, ConcurrentWorkflow, AgentRearrange, GraphWorkflow, MixtureOfAgents, GroupChat, ForestSwarm, HierarchicalSwarm, HeavySwarm and SwarmRouter (a single interface that picks among swarm types). The README also says the project has 60+ multi-agent structures, which we did not count.

**Pros**

- Widest menu of ready-made patterns in this comparison.
- Apache-2.0, 7,403 stars (November 26, 2026), version 17.0.3 on PyPI, pushed on the day we checked.
- SwarmRouter lets you change strategy without rewriting the workflow.

**Cons**

- More patterns means more choices to evaluate, and we did not test any of them.
- Hosted pricing mixes a subscription with a per-token fee (below).
- Premium endpoints are gated behind paid plans.

**Benefits for you.** A fast way to see which shape (sequential, concurrent, hierarchical, graph) fits your problem before you commit to a framework.

**Pricing (as of November 26, 2026, swarms.ai/pricing).** Free $0, Pro $19.99 per month, Premium $100 per month ($1,020 per year), Enterprise custom. New accounts get $5.00 of credit on every plan. Tokens are billed at the model's provider price plus a 15% service fee, with flat fees on top, including $0.01 per agent on swarm, graph and grid workflows. Pro adds multi-agent architectures, Premium Endpoints and priority support; Premium adds higher rate limits (2,000 requests per minute) and SOC 2 compliance.

**Free tier.** Yes: the open source library is free, and the hosted Free plan is $0 with sign-up credit. The pricing page says Premium Endpoints (batch agent and swarm completions, reasoning agents, grid and graph workflows) are not available on Free.

**Limits.** Billable endpoints need a balance above $1.00 and return a 402 below it.

**Standout use case.** HeavySwarm for research and analysis work, per the README's own "best for" column.

**Skip it if** you want one well-documented pattern rather than a catalogue, or you cannot accept a third-party service fee on top of model prices.

**Setup.**

```bash
pip3 install -U swarms
```

### 9. Kimi Work

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/kimi-work.png" name="Kimi Work" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A general-purpose local agent for knowledge workers, available in the "Work" mode of the Kimi desktop client. Kimi's help center says it launched on June 3, 2026 and is in Beta. It sits on Kimi Code and the Kimi K3 model, and it can switch between two execution modes, "Agent" and "Agent Swarm".

**Who it is for.** Non-developers who want organised files, analysed data and finished documents from a desktop app, and who occasionally have a task big enough for a swarm.

**Key features (Kimi help center and product page).**

- Agent Swarm mode that, by Kimi's description, assembles a team of up to 300 sub-agents based on task complexity (vendor-reported).
- Skills (including third-party and uploaded local Skills), scheduled tasks, widgets, a dashboard and plugins for apps such as Canva, Notion and WPS.
- A browser extension for browser operation.
- Three permission levels: default, manual approval and fully automatic.

**Pros**

- No terminal, no config files and no framework to learn.
- It is a local agent, so it works on files on your machine.
- Its help center says it can install and use Skills, recommends third-party Skills and accepts uploaded local Skills.

**Cons**

- Beta, and Kimi says it iterates frequently.
- Desktop only: the help center says Mac (Apple silicon, macOS 12 or later) and Windows 10 or later, while the product page lists an Intel Mac download too. The two pages disagree, so check the download page before you commit.
- The "fully automatic" permission level runs without asking, which is a real risk on local files.

**Benefits for you.** It puts swarm-style parallelism on your own files without a developer toolchain.

**Pricing.** We found no separate Kimi Work price on the pages we read. The Kimi membership tiers are listed in the Kimi Agent Swarm section above, but neither Kimi Work page states which tier it needs or what the Free plan allows, so we do not claim one. (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Not stated.

**Limits.** Beta; platform requirements above.

**Standout use case.** Kimi's own examples are moving information around, organising files, analysing data and producing reports.

**Skip it if** you are a developer who wants a scriptable swarm, or your files are subject to rules that forbid a third-party agent touching them.

### 10. swarm-ide

{% logo src="https://raw.githubusercontent.com/devanshug2307/agentskills-blog-content/main/images/best-agent-swarm-tools-2026/logos/swarm-ide.png" name="swarm-ide" size=40 /%}

**What it is.** A community project (chmod777john/swarm-ide) described as "An IDE built for Agent Swarms", supporting Kimi K2.5, GLM-4.7 and other models. Its README says the models do not need K2.5's reinforcement learning to work with it. It was created on January 1, 2026 and the last push we saw was March 2, 2026.

**Who it is for.** Builders who want to see an agent swarm: topology graph, live message flow and each agent's context, rather than trust a black box.

**Key features (README, Chinese and English).**

- The only two primitives are create and send: any agent can create sub-agents and message any other agent.
- A graph view of swarm topology and live communication, a tree-style conversation list so you can talk to any agent at any depth, and a panel showing each agent's LLM history.
- MCP configuration through `mcp.json` or `.mcp.json`, supporting stdio, http and sse servers.
- Skill support: it scans a `skills/` directory and can auto-inject a skill into new agents with `auto-load: true` in the `SKILL.md` frontmatter.

**Pros**

- The clearest window into what a swarm is doing, in a free, readable project.
- Reads standard `SKILL.md` files and MCP config, which suits the agentskills.codes catalog.
- 1,497 stars as of November 26, 2026.

**Cons**

- No license is declared on the repository, which matters if you want to build on it.
- No push since March 2, 2026, so treat it as a reference, not a dependency.
- The README's comparison table claims advantages over Kimi's swarm and Claude agent teams. That table is the author's own, and we did not verify it.
- Needs Docker and Bun, runs on Linux, and uses an OpenRouter key.

**Benefits for you.** Reading it teaches you what the create-and-send primitives look like in a working swarm, which helps you judge the commercial tools.

**Pricing.** Free code. You pay your model provider (the author says they use OpenRouter with Kimi 2.5). (Checked November 26, 2026.)

**Free tier.** Yes.

**Limits.** Linux, Docker, Bun, an OpenRouter key.

**Standout use case.** The README's own starter prompt (translated from Chinese): ask it to create three children, message each, and have each create three grandchildren.

**Skip it if** you need a maintained, licensed dependency for production.

**Setup (from the README).**

```bash
cd swarm-ide/backend
cp .env.example .env.local   # add your key and model
docker compose up -d
curl -X POST http://127.0.0.1:3017/api/admin/init-db
bun install
bun dev
```

{% figure src="https://opengraph.githubassets.com/1/chmod777john/swarm-ide" alt="GitHub social card for the chmod777john/swarm-ide repository." caption="GitHub's generated social card for the swarm-ide repository. Source: GitHub, chmod777john/swarm-ide" /%}

### Selection notes

The topic brief flagged Hermes Kanban, Kimi Work and swarm-ide for verification. Hermes Kanban has first-party docs and launch posts, Kimi Work has a Kimi help-center page and a product page, and swarm-ide has a public GitHub repository, so all three stayed. One related item left out on purpose: OpenAI's original Swarm repository. Its README says it is replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK, so it appears in the definition section and in item 4 rather than as a separate product.

