## Best for X: decision table

| If you need | Pick | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Parallel code review or research in a terminal | Claude Code subagents | Stable feature, summaries only return to the main context |
| Teammates that challenge each other | Claude Code agent teams | Direct messages and a shared task list, but experimental |
| A huge parallel research job with no code | Kimi Agent Swarm | Published ceiling of 300 sub-agents (vendor-reported) |
| Restartable, auditable multi-agent pipelines | Hermes Kanban | Every handoff is a SQLite row, with comments and unblocking |
| Routing a conversation between specialists | OpenAI Agents SDK | Handoffs are tools, with approvals |
| Role-based crews with a free hosted tier | CrewAI | Sequential or hierarchical process, Basic free |
| Explicit control over who talks to whom | Agency Swarm | Communication flows declared in code |
| To compare many swarm shapes quickly | Swarms | Ten architectures in the README table |
| A swarm on your desktop files, no terminal | Kimi Work | Beta desktop app with an Agent Swarm mode |
| To see what a swarm is doing | swarm-ide | Graph, message flow and each agent's context |

### Common mistakes

- **Starting with a swarm.** Anthropic's own docs say sequential tasks, same-file edits and dependency-heavy work are better in a single session or subagents.
- **Forgetting that enabling teams changes subagent behavior.** While teams are enabled in Claude Code, a named subagent launches as a teammate.
- **Letting nesting grow.** Hermes documents that depth 3 with 3 children each can reach 27 concurrent leaf agents, and Claude Code subagents can nest three layers by default.
- **Trusting vendor numbers.** The 300 sub-agent and 3x to 4.5x figures are Kimi's own and we could not reproduce them.
- **Treating a stale community repo as a dependency.** swarm-ide has no license and no push since March 2, 2026.

### Setup checklist

1. Write the task as independent pieces. If you cannot, use one agent.
2. Set a spend ceiling before you start: a plan tier, a depth limit or a credit pool.
3. Prefer subagents or an orchestrator-worker pattern before peer teams.
4. Decide where the audit trail lives (transcripts, a board, traces).
5. Keep write access narrow: read-only workers for research, one writer per file.
6. Reread the vendor limits page the week you ship. Prices and flags in this space move fast.

## How we evaluated

We read first-party documentation and pricing pages, pulled repository facts from the GitHub API, checked package versions on PyPI, and verified every embedded video and post. All fetches are dated November 26, 2026 unless noted.

What we did not do: we did not run Claude Code agent teams, Kimi Agent Swarm, Kimi Work, Hermes Kanban or any SDK, and we did not run the catalog skills. There are no benchmarks of our own. Every performance number is vendor-reported, and we flag it. The only calls we made were HTTP requests: documentation fetches, GitHub and PyPI API reads, oEmbed and X syndication checks, and status checks on links and images. Where two vendor pages disagree (Hermes default child concurrency, Kimi Work Intel Mac support) we say so instead of picking one.

## FAQ

### What does agent swarm mean?

There is no single definition. In current usage it covers three designs: an orchestrator that spawns many workers (Kimi Agent Swarm, Claude Code subagents), a team that shares a task list and messages each other (Claude Code agent teams, Hermes Kanban), and a handoff chain where one specialist at a time owns the conversation (OpenAI Agents SDK).

### Is an agent swarm the same as a multi-agent system?

A swarm is a kind of multi-agent system, but the word usually implies many workers, run-time decisions about how many to spawn, or both. Kimi's help center calls its design "scale-out" with no predefined roles. Sequential pipelines with fixed roles are multi-agent systems that most people would not call swarms.

### How do I use an agent swarm in Claude Code?

For subagents, ask Claude to delegate or add a markdown file in `.claude/agents/`. For agent teams, set `CLAUDE_CODE_EXPERIMENTAL_AGENT_TEAMS=1` and ask for a team. The @bcherny post called teams "Agent Swarms in Claude Code" on February 5, 2026, but Anthropic's docs call them agent teams and mark them experimental.

### What is the difference between Claude Code subagents and agent teams?

Subagents run inside your session and report results back to the main agent. Agent teams are separate Claude Code instances that share a task list and message each other directly. Teams cost significantly more tokens and are disabled by default. Anthropic's rule of thumb: subagents for quick workers that report back, teams when workers need to challenge each other.

### How many sub-agents does Kimi Agent Swarm use?

Kimi says up to 300 sub-agents and more than 4,000 tool calls in a single task, in its help center's K2.6 description. These are vendor-reported. Your plan limits how many subtasks run at once: 2 on Plus, 4 on Pro, 8 on Max and 8 on Ultra.

### What does "kimi agent集群" mean?

集群 means cluster in Chinese, and "agent集群" is the Chinese search phrase for what Kimi calls Agent Swarm in English. The swarm-ide README uses the Chinese word 蜂群 (literally "bee swarm") for the same idea. The Kimi feature is the same product either way.

### Is Kimi Agent Swarm free?

Kimi's membership page lists a $0 Free plan, but its swarm concurrency row is blank for Free and starts at 2 on Plus. The Plus plan is described as covering Agent Swarm. Treat the swarm as a paid feature unless Kimi states otherwise on the day you read this.

### Are agent swarms more expensive than a single agent?

Usually. Anthropic says agent teams use significantly more tokens than a single session, and token usage scales with active teammates. Kimi's credits are shared across features. Hermes warns that nested delegation multiplies spend. The cost buys speed and breadth, not necessarily better answers.

### What is the best open source agent swarm framework?

It depends on the pattern. CrewAI has the most stars of the frameworks here (61,301). The OpenAI Agents SDK is the cleanest for handoffs. Google ADK has the widest choice of fixed and dynamic workflows. Swarms has the most prebuilt structures. Hermes Kanban is the one built around a durable board.

### What is the difference between a handoff and agents as tools?

In the OpenAI Agents SDK, a handoff transfers the conversation: the specialist becomes the active agent. With agents as tools, a manager keeps control and calls specialists for bounded subtasks. The docs say you can combine them.

### What is the difference between Hermes Kanban and `delegate_task`?

Hermes' own docs: `delegate_task` runs child agents inside one conversation and returns their summaries, while Kanban is a work queue where every handoff is a row any profile or human can read. Use delegation when the result belongs in the parent's context. Use Kanban when work must survive restarts or be picked up by someone else.

### Is OpenAI's Swarm library still maintained?

No. Its README says Swarm is replaced by the OpenAI Agents SDK, the production-ready evolution, and recommends migrating for production use.

### Do these tools work with agent skills and MCP servers?

Partly, and it varies. Kimi Work's help center says it supports installing and using Skills. The swarm-ide README documents both `SKILL.md` auto-loading and MCP config. The OpenAI Agents SDK documents MCP servers, including approval settings. For the other tools, check their current docs before assuming. The catalog block above lists entries to start from.

### When should I not use an agent swarm?

When the task is sequential, touches one file, has many dependencies, or fits in one context window. Anthropic's docs say so about agent teams, and the same logic applies to the others.

